\f4\i\fs29\fsmilli14667 \cf3 \strokec3 User prompt: No I want you to search the internet and tell me whether this is possible. I was thinking of what would be the leanest and most cost-effective way to deploy agentic AI to perform tasks such as this. Firstly, deploy the agentic AI to go on websites such as bid.cars and Copart or IAAI to search for vehicles that fit our particular criteria in terms of damage, probably using tools such as vision model to take a screenshot of that particular car and see the extent of the damage in order to classify whether it is acceptable by us and see whether it would be a good deal. After the agent identifies that, so it will then post those images onto our Twitter page and list a potential vehicle. Alternatively, instead of focusing just on websites like bid.cars, we could focus on aggregate websites for dealerships such as cars.com or AutoTrader that will go out, search for particular vehicles that are popular within Nigeria, take the details of the cars, possibly search online using tools that give a breakdown of a vehicle's or a car's spec sheet. For instance, if it's a Mercedes G-Wagon, it goes into tools that can tell what options the vehicle has such as maybe the Night Package II or maybe carbon fiber, and then identify compared to the current market prices and compared to maybe our already existing database whether this would be a great deal for us or for another client. And then, once it identifies that from those aggregate sites, it then sends it maybe to another agent to make the post on Twitter, which will include the images, etc., and whatnot after it has extracted the images from the source. What costs would we be looking at if we were to use models such as Gemini K3 or Opus 4.8 or 5, and how quick and realistic would that be, and would it be able to get past things such as Cloudflare human verification or CAPTCHA verification check up modern agentic AI capabilities.
\f3\i0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa426\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs29\fsmilli14667 \cf0 Response: This pipeline is entirely technically possible, but deploying a pure "autonomous agent" to click around auction sites with a live browser will burn cash and trigger anti-bot walls.
\f3\fs24 \

